# G035 — Chat Application (Networking)

*v2. See `chat-app.md` for prose: first multi-party choreography,
pub-sub with dynamic membership, message routing as published to
subscribed channel, history as scoped journal, join/leave as
lifecycle primitives.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Executive Ghost Coordination

[exec-coordination
    IN ORDER TO
        route project-scoped conversations through named rooms
        letting agents join and leave mid-flight
        with every message persisted as a journal entry per room

    @srv <- [chat[new-server]]

    [chat[create-room {server: @srv, name: "operations"}]]
    [chat[create-room {server: @srv, name: "editorial"}]]

    concurrent [
        kathryn:
            [chat[join {server: @srv, user: "kathryn",
                         room: "operations"}]]
            [chat[send {server: @srv, room: "operations",
                         sender: "kathryn",
                         text: "Q2 budget posted"}]]
    |
        eliana:
            [chat[join {server: @srv, user: "eliana",
                         room: "operations"}]]
            @history <- [chat[get-messages
                                {server: @srv,
                                 room: "operations",
                                 since: [clock[now]] - 3600000}]]
    |
        sylvia:
            [chat[join {server: @srv, user: "sylvia",
                         room: "editorial"}]]
            [chat[send {server: @srv, room: "editorial",
                         sender: "sylvia", text: "new issue drafted"}]]
    ]
    join
]

---

## Verb Surface

[chat
    new-server
    create-room          {server, name}
    join                 {server, user, room}
    leave                {server, user, room}
    send                 {server, room, sender, text}
    get-messages         {server, room, since}
    members              {server, room}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: room as pub-sub channel `where` members subscribe

[chat[send {server: @s, room: @r, sender: @sender, text: @t}]
    IN ORDER TO
        fan out @t to every current member of @r
        while appending to @r's scoped journal under a monotonic seq

    @room <- @s:rooms at @r
    acquire @room:lock
        @seq <- ++@room:counter
        @msg <- {seq, sender, text, ts: now}
        append @msg to @room:messages                ;; journal
        for @member in @room:members
            append @msg to @member:inbox              ;; queue
    release
]

Same discipline as G067's threading chat — many-to-many broadcast
under a per-room lock. G035 is the networking framing; G067 is the
concurrency framing. Both use the room-as-channel primitive.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: history scoped to room `where` since bounds

[chat[get-messages {server: @s, room: @r, since: @t}]
    @room:messages
        where ts is at least @t
    IN ORDER TO
        let a late-joining agent catch up without replaying
        the entire history since the room was created
]

Join-time-forward is the default (members' inboxes don't backfill),
but the journal is always queryable — one room's history is that
room's journal, independent of membership at any given moment.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @s <- [chat[new-server]]
    [chat[create-room {server: @s, name: "ops"}]]
    [chat[join {server: @s, user: "alice", room: "ops"}]]
    [chat[join {server: @s, user: "bob",   room: "ops"}]]

    [chat[send {server: @s, room: "ops",
                 sender: "alice", text: "hi"}]]
    [chat[send {server: @s, room: "ops",
                 sender: "bob",   text: "hi alice"}]]

    -> [chat[get-messages {server: @s, room: "ops", since: 0}]]
    ;; -> [(seq:0, alice, "hi"), (seq:1, bob, "hi alice")]

    [chat[leave {server: @s, user: "bob", room: "ops"}]]
    -> [chat[members {server: @s, room: "ops"}]]    ;; -> {alice}
]
